body:
Monitoring is an important part of a so-called "DevOps" lifecycle, just as much as planning tasks, developing your application and testing it. Yet developers often tend to set this aspect aside. So how do you expect to improve your applications if you haven't monitored them properly? It's not just about configuring your dashboard: you have to feed it content, logs!

If you are a .NET developer, you are no doubt familiar with the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging` NuGet package, which includes a set of interfaces and base classes for writing logs with the provided generic host. Other packages let you add providers that give us the option to persist (or simply display) the entries in other ways (log4net, …).

The approach I come across most often for writing logs with the Microsoft package is using the extension methods `LogTrace`, `LogDebug`, `LogInformation`, `LogError` and `LogCritical`.

In this article, we won't explain categorisation (names, ids, `BeginScope`), the different levels (debug, information, ...) or go into the details of log configuration; we'll look at the "LoggerMessage" pattern and the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.LoggerMessage` class, and then see how to generate code for our logs with the `LoggerMessageAttribute` attribute, found in the same package.

```csharp
namespace Bar;

public class Foo(ILogger<Foo> logger)
{
    private readonly ILogger<Foo> _logger = logger;

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Hello logs!");
        _logger.LogInformation("Hello {Person}", person);
    }

    static Person person = new("John", "Smith");
}

public record Person(string FirstName, string LastName)
{
    public override string ToString() => $"{FirstName} {LastName}";
}
```

In this simple code snippet, we injected the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger` interface to get an instance whose category is the full name of the class passed as a parameter, `Bar.Foo`.

In the `DoSomething` method, we logged two "information" events, each containing a message, without and with a parameter.

# The LoggerMessage pattern

The `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.LoggerMessage` class exposes methods for creating static delegates through the `System.Action<in T1, in T2, in T3, …>` class. These delegates perform better than the `LogTrace`, `LogDebug`, `LogInformation`, etc. extension methods. The main reason? The classic extension methods evaluate message templates every time a log is written. The `LoggerMessage` approach evaluates the message template only once, when it is defined.

In the example above, calling the `DoSomething` method writes the information `Hello logs!` and `Hello {Person}!` to our application's logs, information with and without parameters. Our extension method's role will be to turn our `Person` record into a string.

To use the pattern, we'll make a few changes to our code:

- In a static class dedicated to logging, we'll define two private fields of delegate type `Action<ILogger, Exception>` and `Action<ILogger, string, Exception>` (named `_helloLogs` and `_helloPerson` respectively).
- The first generic parameter must be of type `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger` and the last of type `System.Exception`. In between, we can add as many generic parameters as needed, depending on the number of parameters in the message template passed.
- In a static constructor, for each log, we'll define the log level, an id and its message template using the static method `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.LoggerMessage.Define` and its overloads.
- Finally, to access this mechanism and write the message to the logs, we'll develop two extension methods for the `ILogger` interface.

The logging code should now look something like this snippet:

```csharp
namespace Foo;

public class LoggerExtensions
{
    private static readonly Action<ILogger, Exception?> _helloLogs;
    private static readonly Action<ILogger, string, Exception?> _helloPerson;

    static LoggerExtensions()
    {
        _helloLogs = LoggerMessage.Define(
            LogLevel.Information,
            0,
            "Hello logs!");

        _helloPerson = LoggerMessage.Define<string>(
            LogLevel.Information,
            1,
            "Hello {Person}");
    }

    public static void HelloLogs(this ILogger logger)
        => _helloLogs(logger, null);

    public static void HelloPerson(this ILogger logger, Person person)
        => _helloPerson(logger, person.ToString());
}
```

Now we'll call our extension methods in a new version of our `Bar.Foo.DoSomething` method:

```csharp
namespace Bar;

public class Foo(ILogger<Foo> logger)
{
    private readonly ILogger<Foo> _logger = logger;

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        _logger.HelloLogs();

        Person person = new("John", "Smith");
        _logger.HelloPerson(person);
    }
}
```

# Generating log statements at compile time

.NET 5 introduced "source generators" and version 6 of the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging` package introduced the `LoggerMessageAttribute` type. The attribute generates high-performance logs when the compiler detects it. The generated source code relies on the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger` interface and the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.LoggerMessage` class.

If you don't know how a source generator works:

1. Compilation runs, and our code will be rewritten to MSIL;
2. Among the steps of the Roslyn compiler (used by default in .NET), one of them consists of analysing potential elements to generate;
3. Roslyn will detect the project references, classes, use of attributes, etc. that define source generation;
4. The compiler will generate the sources;
5. The generated code is added to the current compilation;
6. Compilation continues normally with the new elements.

![How source generators work](/images/blog/source-generators-en.svg)

In a static and partial class (an important detail), we'll define the signature of two partial extension methods for the `ILogger` interface, here named `HelloLogs` and `HelloPerson`. The second will take a parameter of type `Person` in order to pass an object into the message template.

# Execution time

Using the `BenchmarkDotNet` NuGet package, I wrote a program to measure the execution time of the three implementations (standard, with the `LoggerMessage` pattern and with the `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.LoggerMessageAttribute` attribute):

```csharp
public class LoggingBenchmarks
{
    private StandardLogging _standardLogging;
    private OptimisedLogging _optimisedLogging;
    private OptimisedLoggingWithAttributes _optimisedLoggingWithAttributes;

    [GlobalSetup]
    public void Setup()
    {
        IServiceCollection serviceCollection = new ServiceCollection();
        serviceCollection.AddLogging(builder => builder.AddFilter("LoggingBenchmarks", LogLevel.Information));

        var loggerFactory = serviceCollection.BuildServiceProvider().GetService<ILoggerFactory>();
        var logger = loggerFactory.CreateLogger("TEST");

        _standardLogging = new StandardLogging(logger);
        _optimisedLogging = new OptimisedLogging(logger);
        _optimisedLoggingWithAttributes = new OptimisedLoggingWithAttributes(logger);
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public void UsingStandardLogging()
    {
        if (_standardLogging is not null)
        {
            _standardLogging.HelloLogs();
            _standardLogging.HelloPerson(person);
        }
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public void UsingOptimisedLogging()
    {
        if (_optimisedLogging is not null)
        {
            _optimisedLogging.HelloLogs();
            _optimisedLogging.HelloPerson(person);
        }
    }

    [Benchmark]
    public void UsingOptimisedLoggingWithAttributes()
    {
        if (_optimisedLoggingWithAttributes is not null)
        {
            _optimisedLoggingWithAttributes.HelloLogs2();
            _optimisedLoggingWithAttributes.HelloPerson2(person);
        }
    }

    static Person person = new Person("John", "Doe");
}

[MemoryDiagnoser]
public class StandardLogging
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public StandardLogging(ILogger logger) => _logger = logger;

    public void HelloLogs() => _logger.LogInformation("Hello logs!");
    public void HelloPerson(Person person) => _logger.LogInformation("Hello {Person}!", person);
}

[MemoryDiagnoser]
public class OptimisedLogging
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public OptimisedLogging(ILogger logger) => _logger = logger;

    public void HelloLogs() => _logger.HelloLogs();
    public void HelloPerson(Person person) => _logger.HelloPerson(person);
}

[MemoryDiagnoser]
public class OptimisedLoggingWithAttributes
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public OptimisedLoggingWithAttributes(ILogger logger) => _logger = logger;

    public void HelloLogs2() => _logger.HelloLogs2();
    public void HelloPerson2(Person person) => _logger.HelloPerson2(person);
}

[MemoryDiagnoser]
public static class Logging
{
    private static readonly Action<ILogger, Exception?> _helloLogs;
    private static readonly Action<ILogger, string, Exception?> _helloPerson;

    static Logging()
    {
        _helloLogs = LoggerMessage.Define(
            LogLevel.Information,
            new EventId(1),
            "Hello logs!");

        _helloPerson = LoggerMessage.Define<string>(
            LogLevel.Information,
            new EventId(2),
            "Hello {Person}!");
    }

    public static void HelloLogs(this ILogger logger)
        => _helloLogs(logger, null);

    public static void HelloPerson(this ILogger logger, Person person)
        => _helloPerson(logger, person.ToString(), null);
}

[MemoryDiagnoser]
public static partial class Logging2
{
    [LoggerMessage(EventId = 0, Level = LogLevel.Critical, Message = "Hello logs! (2)")]
    public static partial void HelloLogs2(this ILogger logger);

    [LoggerMessage(EventId = 0, Level = LogLevel.Critical, Message = "Hello {Person}! (2)")]
    public static partial void HelloPerson2(this ILogger logger, Person person);
}

public record Person(string FirstName, string LastName)
{
    public override string ToString() => $"{FirstName} {LastName}";
}
```

When we decompile the generated source code with JetBrains dotPeek, we get the following result:

```csharp
[MemoryDiagnoser(true)]
public static class Logging2
{
    [GeneratedCode("Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Generators", "7.0.7.1805")]
    private static readonly Action<ILogger, Exception?> __HelloLogs2Callback = LoggerMessage.Define(
        LogLevel.Information,
        new EventId(200, "HelloLogs2"),
        "Hello logs!",
        new LogDefineOptions()
        {
            SkipEnabledCheck = true
        });

    [GeneratedCode("Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Generators", "7.0.7.1805")]
    private static readonly Action<ILogger, Person, Exception?> __HelloPerson2Callback = LoggerMessage.Define<Person>(
        LogLevel.Information,
        new EventId(201, "HelloPerson2"),
        "Hello {Person}!",
        new LogDefineOptions()
        {
            SkipEnabledCheck = true
        });

    [LoggerMessage(EventId = 200, Level = LogLevel.Information, Message = "Hello logs!")]
    [GeneratedCode("Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Generators", "7.0.7.1805")]
    public static void HelloLogs2(this ILogger logger)
    {
        if (!logger.IsEnabled(LogLevel.Information))
        {
            return;
        }

        Logging2.__HelloLogs2Callback(logger, (Exception)null);
    }

    [LoggerMessage(EventId = 201, Level = LogLevel.Information, Message = "Hello {Person}!")]
    [GeneratedCode("Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Generators", "7.0.7.1805")]
    public static void HelloPerson2(this ILogger logger, Person person)
    {
        if (!logger.IsEnabled(LogLevel.Information))
        {
            return;
        }

        Logging2.__HelloPerson2Callback(logger, person, (Exception)null);
    }
}
```

When we run the benchmark with the `dotnet run -c Release` command, we can see that the execution time is divided by 3 when using the pattern, and by almost 10 when using the `LoggerMessage` attribute. I couldn't get the memory allocated by each operation when using the attribute, probably because these are sources generated on the fly.

| Method | Mean | Error | StdDev | Gen0 | Allocated |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| UsingStandardLogging | 59.937 ns | 0.7389 ns | 0.6911 ns | 0.0153 | 32 B |
| UsingOptimisedLogging | 18.710 ns | 0.1788 ns | 0.1672 ns | 0.0119 | 32 B |
| UsingOptimisedLoggingWithAttributes | 6.332 ns | 0.0178 ns | 0.0167 ns | - | - |

# Conclusion

Now imagine your applications and their performance! You no longer have any excuse not to log their events properly. Thanks to this approach, your applications in the cloud (consumption plan) will take less time to run and you'll save some money.
